---
title: Culling what can’t be reached
---
Find the parts of a map that can’t be reached, then delete them or put them to use [[t:222-223 t:230-234 s:24]].

## The rule
1. Find the open tile nearest the center of the map [[c16:36-54]].
2. Make a Dijkstra map from that tile: every tile it can walk to gets its number of steps [[c16:62-63 t:214-219]].
3. Open tiles left without a number can’t be reached [[c16:64-69 t:226-228]].
4. By mode:
   - `mark`: color those tiles and leave them open [[c16:64-69]];
   - `remove`: turn them to wall [[c17:55-65]].

@@diagram cull-flood alt="A small cave with one tile numbered 0. Every tile that can be walked to from it carries its number of steps, up to 5. A pocket of two open tiles at the right is walled off; it has no number and is marked." caption="A Dijkstra map from one tile (0): each tile it can walk to gets its number of steps. The pocket at the right gets none, so it can’t be reached (small squares)." key="0:point"
