- **Talk says,** of Dijkstra maps: start from one or more points and mark the rest of the map with a sentinel value, meaning you can’t go there. Then number every walkable tile by its steps from the start [[t:214-221 s:23]].

## Step through it
@@steps limit=4

## Controls
@@control mode
`mark`: shows the unreachable tiles and leaves them, as the demo `c16-unreachable` does [[c16:62-73]]. `remove`: turns them to wall, as the demo `c17-starting-left` does before it places its start [[c17:54-94]].

## What it produces, measured
@@measured

- **Mark:** changes no tile, so this is the cave map’s own row [[own]].
- **Remove:** {{c17.regions.max}} region on every seed [[run:c17.regions.max]].
- **What it costs:** varies. Floor share after the cull: {{c17.floor_share.min}} to {{c17.floor_share.max}}. The caves before it: never below {{c03.floor_share.min}} [[run:c17.floor_share.min c17.floor_share.max c03.floor_share.min]].
- **Talk says:** culling before picking a start avoids the extreme case, a map with only two tiles you can walk on [[t:236-240]].

## Where it fails
@@fails

- **The cave map’s failures:** marking leaves them in place [[own]].

## What it combines with
@@combines

- **Before a start:** the talk culls before it picks one, so the player can’t be placed somewhere they can’t get out of [[t:236-237]].
- **Or put them to use:** in a game that encourages tunneling, the talk hides things in the unreachable areas [[t:231-234]].

